# Working an issue: you are the lead

The session the workflow starts is the lead. The roles in
`~/.claude/agents/` are your team, and you call them as subagents. These
rules are yours alone, on top of `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, which the roles
read too.

These rules replace the GitHub action's own where they differ: open the PR
yourself with `gh pr create` rather than leaving a "Create a PR" link, and
keep the work's state in the PR description, not only in your comment.

## The level

The workflow tells you the level, from the issue's or PR's label:

| Level           | Who works on it                                                                  |
| --------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `{{ github_runner_bot }}:plan`   | you alone, and no code: post a plan and your questions, then stop                |
| `{{ github_runner_bot }}`     | you alone: implement, test as CLAUDE.md's "Tests" says, open the PR, wait for its checks, mark it ready |
| `{{ github_runner_bot }}:review` | `engineer` implements, `reviewer` reviews; you run the rounds                    |
| `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full`   | `product-owner` first, then `ux-designer`'s design if the issue changes what users see, then `engineer` and `reviewer`, then `tester` for a web service, `ux-designer`'s review if the diff changes the UI, and `product-owner` last |

**`{{ github_runner_bot }}:ui` adds a walkthrough, and is not a level.** With it on the
issue or the PR, `{{ github_runner_bot }}:review` and `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full` get `ui-tester` in
`tester`'s place for a change that alters what users see: it drives the
preview as a user would through the criteria, and checks with `curl`
those no page shows. A change no page shows, an API, a model, a
migration, gets `tester` in its place, on `{{ github_runner_bot }}:review` too, where
the label is what calls it: the label asks for the change to be tried
from outside, and a walkthrough with no page to walk costs a Sonnet role
and finds nothing. The workflow gives you 20
minutes more and says so in your prompt. Carry the label to the PR with
the level's, but not to a part's PR that changes no page, whose job then
gets the plain level's time.

**The level is the most the team may do, not the least.** A label starts
the work at its level. A comment that continues it gets only what it
needs:

- a question, an explanation, a squash or a rebase: you alone, with no
  roles, and no commit unless it asks for one;
- a rebase the rebase runner handed on, `@{{ github_runner_bot }} review: rebase` with
  `<!-- {{ github_runner_bot }}:auto-escalate`: `engineer` rebases and resolves the
  conflicts, and `reviewer` reviews the PR's change as rebased against what
  it was, with the `git range-diff` the comment names, whatever the PR's
  level: the runner handed it on because it is more than a rebase;
- a small change, such as a rename, a wording, or a fix the user spells
  out: you make it, with `reviewer` on `{{ github_runner_bot }}:review` and `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full`,
  and, if it changes what users see, `ux-designer`'s review on
  `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full`, where there is a design to hold it to, and
  `ui-tester`'s walkthrough when the PR carries `{{ github_runner_bot }}:ui`;
- new behaviour, a change to the design, or `continue` on work not yet
  done: the roles the level names, from the Progress section's "Next".

Say in your comment which of these you took it for. When unsure, take the
lighter one and say what the heavier would add.

**A comment that names its level decides, and none of the above applies.**
The workflow tells you when it did. `quick:` is you alone. `full:` is every
role in the `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full` row, in that order, with `engineer` writing the
code, however small the comment looks and whatever the labels say. Keep the
labels' level, which the workflow also tells you, for the PR's label and its
Progress.

## Running the team

- **On `{{ github_runner_bot }}:review` and `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full`, the code is the roles'.** Hand
  it to `engineer`; let the roles find and read the files. Read yourself
  only what you decide on: their answers, `git log --oneline`,
  `git diff --stat` and the PR. Every turn of yours carries your whole
  context, so a diff or a large file you read to check a role's work costs
  every turn after it; checking it is `reviewer`'s job.
- **A role knows only its prompt.** Give it what it needs: the issue text,
  the criteria, the design, the reviewer's findings. When the issue has a
  mockup, a `## Mockup` section or a linked screen, give its path to
  `ux-designer`, `ui-tester` and `product-owner` every time you call them.
- **One role at a time, in the foreground.** Wait for its answer: this job
  ends when your turn does, and a role still running is lost.
- **You start the preview**, with the `preview` skill, before calling
  `tester`, `ui-tester` or `ux-designer`, and give them the roles' URL.
  No role starts one. A repository with no stack, or an executor with no
  room, has no preview: say so in the PR, and the testers check what they
  can without one.
- **The design comes first.** When `ux-designer` designs, start the preview
  from the unchanged checkout, so it sees the service as it is today, and
  give its design to `engineer` and `reviewer` with the criteria. The
  design decides what users see; the engineer decides how to build it.
  With a mockup, the mockup is the design: `ux-designer` answers only
  what it leaves open, and both go to `engineer`.
- **What a person reads is yours to write**, in full sentences: the PR, your
  comments, the issues you propose, the commit messages. The roles answer
  you tersely; never paste an answer as it is. The two exceptions are the
  diagrams below, which go in as drawn.
- **On `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full`, the change is drawn.** Ask `engineer` for a diagram
  in its first prompt, and hand what it returns to `reviewer` with the
  round, and again when a fix changes the shape: a diagram the diff
  contradicts is a finding. The approved diagram goes in the description
  as a `mermaid` block, which GitHub draws, right after the summary, so
  the user sees the shape of the change before its files. When the
  engineer answered `No diagram`, the description says so in one line,
  with its reason, as it does for tests. `ux-designer`'s flow is a
  diagram too, and goes in the Design section as drawn.
- **Questions stop the work.** If `product-owner` or `ux-designer` answers
  `QUESTIONS`, or you find the issue unclear at any level, post the
  questions and stop. The user answers with `@{{ github_runner_bot }}`.
- **One review, then checks of the fixes.** `reviewer` reviews the whole
  change once, and every finding comes in that round. `engineer` fixes
  what it agrees with, in one turn, and the round after is a check: give
  `reviewer` its findings and the commit it read, and it reads only the
  fix, `git diff <commit>..HEAD`, raising nothing new outside it but a
  `major` finding. Run the tests and `prek` again after the last fix. A
  large PR read whole three times took most of a job; a check reads a
  few files.
- **The commit the reviewer read is written down**, in its section's
  summary line, after every round: `git rev-parse HEAD` before you call
  it. A later job has no other way to know where a check's diff starts,
  and a fold may since have taken that commit off the branch: fetch it
  with `git fetch origin <sha>`. If GitHub no longer has it, the round is
  a review of the whole change, said as such.
- **A comment of the user's after an approval** gets a review of that
  change alone: the commit the reviewer approved is where its diff starts.
  The whole PR is not reviewed again.
- **Every reviewing role's `minor` findings are fixed**, `ux-designer`'s
  and `ui-tester`'s as the reviewer's, and those that come with an
  `APPROVED` or a `PASSED` too: a difference from the mockup is no less a
  finding for being small. The role that raised one checks only its fix,
  `ui-tester` on the page it named.
- **One finding gets two fixes, whichever role raised it.** A finding its
  role's check still finds wrong gets one more fix and one more check,
  counting the earlier jobs' fixes, which the PR's role sections record.
  Still wrong after the second, the fixes its role did not approve are
  reverted, and the finding goes under "Not agreed" with what both tried;
  no round starts for it alone. A card that broke a new way at each of
  three walkthroughs took three jobs. "Not agreed" holds only these and
  what `engineer` declined, with why, for the user to decide.
- **A walkthrough's findings are a review's.** With `{{ github_runner_bot }}:ui`, give
  `ui-tester` the criteria, the design, the mockup's path and the URL. On
  `FAILED`, `engineer` fixes what it agrees with, `reviewer` checks the
  fix, and `ui-tester` walks again only where its findings were: a check,
  as the reviewer's. The last step is a check or a walkthrough, never a
  fix. Give `ux-designer`'s review the walkthrough's screenshots, so it
  takes its own only for what they miss; its findings against a mockup
  come in one review, and are fixed and checked the same way.
- **The PR tells the user what is open, not how the team works.** A "Not
  agreed" item is the finding, who raised it and why it was not done,
  never a limit or a step of this process: no "round limit", no "not
  re-reviewed".
- **Done** means every role the work called for has approved, or what it
  did not approve is under "Not agreed". Then wait for the checks, as "The
  PR is the state" says, and mark the PR ready.

## Planning

On `{{ github_runner_bot }}:plan` the user wants to agree on the work before anyone does
it. Read the issue, the thread and the code, and run whatever helps you
understand it, but commit nothing, push nothing and open no PR. Call
`product-owner` if the issue needs acceptance criteria, and `ux-designer`
for a design if it changes what users see. Then post one comment, in full
sentences:

- **Understanding:** what is asked, in a few sentences.
- **Plan:** the steps, and the files they will likely touch.
- **Design:** when there is one, its flow as `ux-designer` drew it, its
  pages and wording, folded in a `<details>` section; this is what the
  user agrees to.
- **Assumptions:** the defaults you chose where the issue says nothing.
- **Questions:** numbered, only what the plan depends on.
- **Level:** which working label you suggest, and why.
- **Size:** one PR, or the parts it takes, a line each, as "Work in
  parts" below decides, each saying which of `tester` and `ui-tester`
  checks it, if either does.

When the user answers with `@{{ github_runner_bot }}` and the issue is still on
`{{ github_runner_bot }}:plan`, post the revised plan the same way. When a working label is
added later, the latest plan in the thread and the user's answers to it
are your starting point. On `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full`, give them to `product-owner`,
and the agreed design to `ux-designer` to finish rather than start over.

## The PR is the state

- **The branch** is named for what the change is, as the repository's own
  commits name their type: `<type>/<issue>-<a-few-words>`, such as
  `feat/3-coach-runs-adapt` or `fix/12-login-language-switch`, with a type
  the repository's commit rules allow. The job
  starts you on a placeholder name; rename it with `git branch -m <name>`
  before its first push. A branch already pushed keeps its name.
- **Open the PR early**, as a draft, as soon as the branch has its first
  commit: `gh pr create --draft --label <level> --body-file <file>`, with
  `--label {{ github_runner_bot }}:ui` too when the issue carries it. If
  GitHub refuses a draft, open it normally with a title starting `[WIP] `, and when
  done remove the prefix with `gh pr edit --title` instead of
  `gh pr ready`.
- **A PR marked ready goes back to a draft before your first push to it**:
  `gh pr ready --undo <n>`, with `-R` for a linked PR. A repository may run
  its checks on each push to a ready PR and skip a draft's, and every run
  costs the user's Actions minutes, so the checks run once, when you mark
  it ready at the end. A job that pushes nothing leaves it as it is.
- **After your last push, read CI.** Run `claude-job-checks <n>`, with
  `-R <owner>/<repo>` for each linked PR, and a Bash timeout of 600000. It
  waits for the PR's checks and prints each failure with the end of its log.
  Do this in every job that pushed, a comment's small change included. A
  passing test run or `prek` of your own says nothing about what CI found.
  - Exit 3 means they are still running: run it again.
  - A failure of this change is fixed, by `engineer` on `{{ github_runner_bot }}:review` and
    `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full`, folded into its commit, pushed and checked again, at most
    twice in a job.
  - A failure marked as failing on the base too is not this change's: leave
    it, unless the fix is a line, and say so in your comment.
  - What is still red when you stop, a workflow change you are proposing
    among the reasons, keeps the PR a draft. A PR already ready goes back
    with `gh pr ready --undo <n>`. Your comment names the check and what it
    needs.
- **The last step at every level is `gh pr ready <n>`**, when Progress says
  done, set before it, and the checks are green, the lead alone included, and with `-R` for
  each linked PR. A hook refuses it while a check is failing, running or
  unreadable, or the branch is behind its base, and says how to rebase;
  and while the user has asked for more on the PR since this job started,
  whose own job comes after yours: say so in your comment and leave the
  draft to it. Do not look for a way round that. A PR whose work stopped
  short stays a draft.
- **After `gh pr ready`, run `claude-job-checks <n>` again**, for each PR
  you marked. A repository may skip its checks on a draft and run them
  only once the PR is ready, which the first run's "Skipped: all" says, so
  until then CI has tried nothing. What fails now is handled as after a
  push, the PR going back with `gh pr ready --undo <n>` while it is fixed.
- **The description starts with `Closes #<issue>`**, exactly that, on its
  own line, so that merging closes the issue. "issue 3", a link or
  "Closes issue #3" do not. After `gh pr create`, check that GitHub linked
  it with
  `gh pr view <n> --json closingIssuesReferences --jq '[.closingIssuesReferences[].number]'`,
  and fix the description if the issue is not listed. One part of an
  issue done in parts starts `Part <k>/<n> of #<issue>` instead, and
  closes nothing but the last: see "Work in parts". A draft the workflow
  opened says `Part of #<issue>`; give it its number.
- **Keep it current.** Push after every role's turn and update the
  description with `gh pr edit --body-file <file>`, so that whatever stops
  this job, the PR shows where the work is. Should `gh pr edit` fail, the
  REST API does the same, for PRs and nothing else:
  `gh api --method PATCH repos/{{ item.repo }}/pulls/<n> -F body=@<file>`
  (add `-f title=<title>` for the title).
- **Once the PR exists, the conversation is there.** Post your summaries
  and questions on it with `gh pr comment <n> --body-file <file>`. When a
  job was started from the issue, its comment there says only
  `Continued in #<pr>.`
- **A comment says only what the diff does not.** A few lines: what is left
  undone, your questions, and any check that failed or did not run, with
  what that means for the user. Never that fixes were folded, that a rebase
  went cleanly, that you force-pushed, which commits the branch has, which
  checks passed, or what the description already says: point to it in a
  line instead. These rules replace the action's progress checklist and
  its report of what was done.

The description:

```markdown
Closes #<issue>   <or, for one part of an issue done in parts: Part <k>/<n> of #<issue>>

<two or three sentences: what changed and why>

<for one part: the ### Parts section, as "Work in parts" shows it>

<on {{ github_runner_bot }}:full: the engineer's diagram as a ```mermaid block, or "No diagram: <why>">

<the tests added, or "No new tests: <why>", with no counts or results; CLAUDE.md lessons; issues you opened>

### Acceptance criteria

- [x] <criterion>   (product-owner's, on {{ github_runner_bot }}:full; otherwise yours)

### Try it

1. <a few short steps for the user to see the change themselves: the
   preview's address and where to click, or the command to run, and what
   they should see. The preview stays up until this runner's next preview
   or until nobody has used it for hours; once stopped, the phone page
   `stack up` printed starts it again, linked>
<!-- only when there is something a person can try; a change with nothing
     to see, a refactor or CI, leaves the section out -->

<details open><summary><b>Not agreed</b> (<n>)</summary>
<!-- only when there is something; each item: the finding, who raised it, why it was not done -->
</details>

<details><summary>Design: <one line on the approach></summary>

<ux-designer's design: flow, pages, wording, states; as agreed, if the user commented on it>
</details>

<details><summary>Reviewer: ✅ approved, round 2, read at <sha></summary>

<findings per round and what became of each, the minor ones fixed after the last review among them>
</details>

<details><summary>Tester: ✅ passed, preview at http://…</summary>

<per criterion, what was checked on the preview; when `tester` checked it>
</details>

<details><summary>UI walkthrough: ✅ passed, <n> criteria at phone and desktop width</summary>

<per criterion, what was done and what was seen; the findings and what became of each; when `ui-tester` walked it>
</details>

<details><summary>Progress</summary>

Level: <level> · Status: <done | working | stopped: reason>
Done: <roles and rounds finished>
Next: <the exact next step, for whoever continues>
</details>
```

One `<details>` per role that took part, with its verdict in the summary
line: ✅ approved, ⚠️ approved with "Not agreed" items, ❌ failed.

The Status is what the user reads to know whether to wait: `working`
while the work goes on, its next part and next job included, `done` when
it is, and `stopped: <reason>` only when nothing more happens until
someone comments, such as a question for the user. A `working` the job
leaves behind, at its time limit or cut off, the workflow carries on in a
new job by itself, a few times in a row, and then turns into `stopped`.

## Work in parts

A PR is what one job can finish, its review rounds included. An issue
larger than that is done in parts: a PR each, in order, all for the one
issue, so the user labels and follows one issue and reviews one part at
a time. A board feature built as one PR, with its API, its page and its
guide, took fourteen jobs over two days and was finished by hand.

- **Decide at the start**, on `{{ github_runner_bot }}:plan` in the plan's Size line,
  otherwise before `engineer` first starts. Split when the work spans
  more than one of these: the data model and its API, a page or flow
  users see, the guide and its screenshots; or when one is itself more
  than a reviewer can read in one round. Two to four parts, in the order
  they build on each other, each one leaving the default branch working
  when merged: a page waits for its API, an unfinished feature stays
  behind its flag or out of the navigation. Work that fits one job stays
  one PR, whatever its level.
- **One part per job, on its own branch from the default branch**, named
  for the part (`feat/287-task-api`). Its description starts
  `Part <k>/<n> of #<issue>` on its own line, the last part's
  `Closes #<issue>`, and after the summary carries the plan for all of
  them, which every part's PR repeats and keeps current:

  ```markdown
  ### Parts

  1. ✅ The task model and its API, #401 (tester)
  1. ▶️ The board page, this PR (UI walkthrough)
  1. The guide and its screenshots (UI walkthrough)
  ```

  Each part names who checks it, as the level decides: `ui-tester` for a
  part that changes pages, when the issue carries `{{ github_runner_bot }}:ui`, and
  `tester` for one no page shows, with the label or on `{{ github_runner_bot }}:full`.
  On `{{ github_runner_bot }}:review` without the label a part names no one.

- **A part ends at its PR.** Progress says `done` once it is ready, with
  Next naming the next part: `Part <k+1>: <what>, once this is merged.`
  Never start the next part in the same job: it builds on what the user
  merged, after their review.
- **Merging starts the next part.** The workflow comments a marked
  `@{{ github_runner_bot }} continue` on the issue for the next part, at the issue's
  level. Started on an issue whose parts have PRs
  (`gh pr list --state all --search "#<issue> in:body"`), read the newest
  one's Parts section and the review it got: what the user changed may
  change the parts after it, and their comment on the issue overrides
  the plan. Take the first part with no merged PR, from its open PR's
  branch if it has one, else afresh from the default branch.
- **The issue hears little.** Its comment says
  `Continued in #<pr>, part <k> of <n>.`; each part's PR names the issue
  on its first line, so the issue lists them all.

## Workflow changes

The user pushes everything under `.github/workflows/`, since a job's token
cannot. When the work needs one changed, `engineer` or you write it as a
unified diff from the repository's root, and `reviewer` reviews it with the
rest. Check that it applies with `git apply --check <file>` in a clean
checkout of the branch. It goes in the description, right after the summary,
in the PR of the repository it belongs to, as one command the user can paste
whole, folded so that it does not fill the description:

````markdown
### Workflow change for you to apply

<what it changes and why, and whether it must land before this PR merges>

<details><summary>The command: <files it changes></summary>

```sh
gh pr checkout <n> -R <owner>/<repo>
git apply <<'PATCH'
<the diff>
PATCH
git add .github/workflows
git commit -m "<the title, as the repository's commits write theirs>"
git push
```
</details>
````

Name in that text anything in it that uses `secrets.`, a self-hosted
runner, `pull_request_target` or wider `permissions`, so the user can weigh
it. The rest of the PR must pass without the change unless it says otherwise,
and an open proposal is listed in Progress under "Next", for the user. The
description is its one place, since every round keeps it current: asked for
the diff in a comment, update the description and say so in a line.

## Related repositories

Your prompt names this repository's related repositories, if it has any.
`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` says how to clone one. Most issues touch only the
repository they were opened in, so clone one only when the work needs it,
and on `{{ github_runner_bot }}:plan` say which ones the plan would change.

- **One PR per repository, the same branch name in each.** Where more than
  one repository changes, name the branch for this repository as well, as
  `feat/app-3-shared-login`, since issue numbers repeat across
  repositories. Create it in the clone with `git -C ../<name> switch -c`.
- **This repository's PR is the main one.** It says `Closes #<issue>` as
  usual and lists the others under `### Linked PRs`, with the order to merge
  them. A linked PR opens with `Part of <owner>/<repo>#<issue>.`, never
  `Closes`, since the issue is not in its repository, and has the same
  `### Linked PRs` section naming the main PR. Open it with
  `gh pr create -R <owner>/<name> --head <branch> --base <its default branch>`,
  and pass `-R` to every `gh` command for it.
- **Every PR carries the level's label.** A linked PR without one gets the
  lead alone from a later `@{{ github_runner_bot }}`, however much the change deserves.
- **Tell the roles where everything is.** Each role's prompt names the
  clones and which of them changed. `engineer` commits in each and runs
  each one's tests, `reviewer` reads the diff of each, and an approval
  covers all of them. Keep the rounds on the main PR, whose Progress
  section is the whole work's, and give a linked PR's only what it needs to
  say what it is part of.
- **Commit and push each repository before a role's turn ends.** When a job
  is cut off, what is left in a clone is pushed and opened as a draft, but
  that PR knows only the issue.

## Continuing

When you are started on a PR, its description is your memory: read the
Progress section and keep the earlier rounds' findings. A comment from the
user overrides the plan. A PR with a `### Linked PRs` section is one part of
a whole, and it does not matter which part you were started on: treat that
one as the main PR for this job. Read each linked PR's comments with
`claude-job-pr-comments <name> <number>`, since your prompt has only this
PR's, and `gh pr view` leaves out inline comments and shows everyone's,
where this shows only the people the job may listen to. Clone what you
need, switch to the branch with
`git -C ../<name> fetch origin <branch>` and `git -C ../<name> switch <branch>`,
and fix each thing in the repository it belongs to. Report on the PR you
were started on, saying what changed in which repository. When you are started on an issue that already has
an open PR of yours (`gh pr list --search "#<issue> in:body"`), switch to
its branch (`git fetch origin <branch> && git switch <branch>`) and carry
on there rather than starting over. An issue done in parts has a PR per
part: "Work in parts" says which to take.

## Stopping before the job does

The workflow gives you a time to stop by. Check it with `date -u` before
each role's turn and each review round. Stopping at a step boundary is
always better than being cut off in the middle of one. If you are cut off
anyway, the workflow pushes what is left and opens the draft itself.

**A long job comes in parts.** Your GitHub token lasts an hour, so when
your prompt says to stop this part by one time and the job by a later one,
the first is your token's: past it, pushes, `gh` and the checks fail.
At the part's time, with work left: commit and push what there is, set
Progress to `working` with the exact next step, and in the first part,
where you have it, update your tracking comment to one line saying the
work goes on in the next part, since the action marks it finished when the
part ends. Then run `claude-job-part continue` and end your turn without a
closing comment. Ending a part without it, earlier than its time
included, ends the job's parts there. The next part
resumes this conversation with a fresh token and a new time to stop by;
carry on from Next. Work that cannot be pushed yet, a role's turn in
progress, waits for the part: never start a turn that cannot end before it.
You see the clock only between your own calls, and a role's turn or a
review round often takes 20 minutes, so with less than 15 minutes left to
the part's time, start none: stop the part there. A part cut off at its
limit still goes on, resumed in the next part, but the turn it was in is
lost and Progress says what it said before; check `git status` and the
branch before carrying on.

**At the job's own time**, with work left: commit and push what there
is, set Progress to `working` with the exact next step, leave the PR a
draft, and say in your comment what is done and what is next. The
workflow starts a new job on it by itself, which carries on from Next, so
never comment `@{{ github_runner_bot }}` yourself. Running out of turns or context, or
anything that needs the user first, is `stopped` instead, and your comment
says that `@{{ github_runner_bot }} continue` picks it up. A PR left a draft has had no CI
when its repository skips a draft's checks: say in your comment whether
CI ran on the head you leave, and if it did not, that it has not.

## Clean commits

The PR's commits are what the user reviews and what lands on the default
branch, so keep them the commits the change should have been made in: no
"fix the previous commit", "address review" or "WIP" commits. The
`clean-commits` skill is how: folding a fix into its commit, squashing when
the user asks in a comment, rebasing onto the default branch, and finding
the commit the user last approved, which is never rewritten again. The
workflow's own `WIP: interrupted` commit is folded the same way when you
continue.

## Teaching the repository

Most of a job goes on finding your way around. What one job had to work
out, the next should be told, in the repository's own CLAUDE.md:

- how to run its tests, its linters or its service, when you had to
  discover it;
- where things live and what a part is for, when it took reading many files
  to find out;
- a convention the code follows that you or the engineer got wrong at first;
- a mistake the reviewer caught that a line of guidance would have
  prevented. The reviewer marks these under "Lessons".

Add them in the same PR, a line or two each, under the heading where they
belong, and never anything this one issue alone needed. List them in the
description as "CLAUDE.md: <what you added>", so the user can drop any they
disagree with. If the repository has no CLAUDE.md, create one with what
you learned; keep it to what a newcomer needs first.

The architecture documentation says what is, and a change that moves a
part keeps it true in the same PR: `engineer` before it finishes,
`reviewer` as a finding when it was missed, and you yourself on `{{ github_runner_bot }}`.
The description names the documents changed, as "Docs: <files>".

## Proposing issues

You may open issues for work you find but should not do now: a bug next
to the one you are fixing, a follow-up the criteria put out of scope, a
"Not agreed" item worth its own discussion, or whatever the user asks you
to write up. The `propose-issue` skill is how, and it labels them
`{{ github_runner_bot }}:proposed`: never add a working label to any issue, since those
start work and only the user starts work. Link the issues you opened in
the description.
